"""Original unit-preservation exercise with source-row traceability."""
BODY = '<section class="page-hero task-hero"><p class="eyebrow">FREE AI TOOLS / TABLE CHECK</p><h1>Check units before adding AI table values.</h1><p class="lede">Keep each original number beside its unit and source row. Convert compatible units into a separate column, then add only those checked values. A missing unit needs clarification; grams do not belong in a milliliter subtotal.</p><div class="task-links"><a href="#practice">Try the five rows</a><a href="#answer">Check your answer</a><a href="#saved-file">Check the saved file</a></div></section>\n<article class="prose">\n<h2>Use a small sample in the tool you already have</h2><p>If your free AI chat can accept pasted text, start with the notes below. You can also arrange them manually in a text editor or spreadsheet. This exercise does not require a paid export feature. Check your account\'s available options before choosing a file route; the useful result is a table whose numbers you can trace and verify.</p>\n<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/table-unit-route.svg" width="760" height="640" alt="Keep source value and unit, convert known compatible units separately, then calculate a labelled subtotal."/><figcaption>Preserve the input before normalizing a value.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id="practice">Five invented Cedar supply notes</h2><div data-original="fixture"><p>These are practice notes we wrote for this guide, not measurements from a real workshop. The question is: how much recorded liquid volume is listed? The fourth line is deliberately incomplete; the fifth records a different kind of quantity.</p><blockquote>C1: blue paint, 250 mL.<br>C2: green paint, 0.5 L.<br>C3: white paint, 750 mL.<br>C4: varnish, 40. Unit not recorded.<br>C5: filler, 100 g.</blockquote><p>Do not add 250, 0.5, 750, 40 and 100 as though they described the same unit. Do not infer the varnish unit from nearby paint rows. Preserve C1 through C5 so a correction can name one source line instead of changing the whole table.</p><p>Download the <a href="/assets/practice/cedar-unit-notes.txt" download>input notes</a>, <a href="/assets/practice/cedar-unit-prompt.txt" download>copyable prompt</a> and <a href="/assets/practice/cedar-unit-answer.csv" download>worked CSV answer</a>. Try the input first, then open the answer key. The CSV is plain text with named columns; open it in your intended editor and inspect the imported values.</p></div>\n<h2>Ask for two sets of values</h2><div data-original="prompt"><p>One set holds exactly what the source says. The other holds a checked conversion. Keeping both makes a factor-of-one-thousand error easier to find.</p><pre>Turn the Cedar notes into a CSV with these columns:\nsource_id,item,source_value,source_unit,volume_ml,status\nPreserve the source number and source unit literally.\nFor C4, use UNKNOWN for source_unit.\nUse only this conversion map: mL = value; L = value * 1000.\nLeave volume_ml empty for a missing unit or a non-volume unit.\nSet status to checked_volume, unit_missing or different_dimension.\nReport the known-volume subtotal and the excluded source IDs.\nDo not estimate missing units or convert grams into milliliters.</pre><p>If the answer has only a single “amount” column, ask it to restore the original-value columns before continuing. If it returns a sentence instead of CSV, ask for the same rows with the requested headers. A different layout is repairable; a guessed unit needs to be removed.</p></div>\n<h2 id="answer">The worked answer: 1500 mL of recorded liquid volume</h2><div data-original="answer"><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Original amount</th><th>Volume, mL</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C1, blue paint</td><td>250 mL</td><td>250</td><td>Checked volume</td></tr><tr><td>C2, green paint</td><td>0.5 L</td><td>500</td><td>Checked volume</td></tr><tr><td>C3, white paint</td><td>750 mL</td><td>750</td><td>Checked volume</td></tr><tr><td>C4, varnish</td><td>40; unit missing</td><td>Empty</td><td>Unit missing</td></tr><tr><td>C5, filler</td><td>100 g</td><td>Empty</td><td>Different dimension</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C2 is 0.5 × 1000 = 500 mL. Add the three checked volume rows: 250 + 500 + 750 = 1500 mL, or 1.5 L. Label that result “known-volume subtotal; C4 unresolved, C5 mass excluded.” It is not a complete volume inventory, and it is not an instruction to mix those products.</p><p>The answer key keeps C2\'s source_value as 0.5 and source_unit as L even though volume_ml is 500. C4 still has its original number 40, with UNKNOWN as its unit and an empty normalized field. C5 retains 100 and g; its empty volume field does not mean the filler amount is zero.</p><p>We checked this key locally with Python decimal arithmetic and a CSV read/write round trip. That checks our supplied exercise and file; it does not measure an AI model\'s accuracy or prove a particular spreadsheet import works.</p></div>\n<h2>Use a conversion rule with a defined scope</h2><p><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-volume" rel="noopener">NIST\'s volume reference</a> lists 1000 mL = 1 L. That supports the liter-to-milliliter conversion in this exercise. A gram measures mass, so the same rule cannot convert C5 into a volume. For a different unit, check a suitable reference before adding another rule.</p><p>If you use Google Sheets, its <a href="https://support.google.com/docs/answer/6055540?hl=en" rel="noopener">CONVERT documentation</a> separates unit categories and says a conversion between incompatible categories returns an error. Confirm the function\'s supported unit names and your formula in the saved sheet. We have not tested a Sheets formula for this exercise.</p>\n<h2>Repair the row, then recalculate</h2><div data-original="repair"><p>Suppose an answer shows C2 as 0.5 mL. This is an invented failure example. Compare it with C2\'s source: 0.5 L. Record the fault as “unit changed without scaling the value,” set volume_ml to 500, and retain 0.5 L in the source columns. Leave C1 and C3 alone; multiplying the entire column would corrupt their already-correct values.</p><p>Now suppose a later source note, C4a, confirms “C4 varnish: 40 mL.” Save that clarification separately and note which row it resolves. C4 can then hold volume_ml 40, a checked status and a reference to C4a. The revised subtotal is 250 + 500 + 750 + 40 = 1540 mL. C5 still stays outside the volume subtotal.</p><p>Keep the earlier 1500 mL version and its unresolved status. This lets someone distinguish a new fact from a calculation repair. If the clarification says a different unit, use that actual unit and a verified conversion rather than assuming this worked revision applies.</p></div>\n<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/table-unit-repair.svg" width="760" height="640" loading="lazy" alt="Fix only C2 from 0.5 mL to 500 mL; resolve C4 only after a new source says 40 mL; keep grams outside the volume total."/><figcaption>A correction and a new source fact are different changes.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id="saved-file">Check what survived saving</h2><div data-original="acceptance"><p>Compare the file you reopened, not only the response in the chat. If your tool cannot export CSV, copy the table into your editor and record that manual step. Use these checks before passing the file to someone else.</p><div class="practice-checklist" data-practice-checklist><label><input type="checkbox"/>All five source IDs remain attached to the correct items.</label><label><input type="checkbox"/>C2 still has 0.5 L in the source columns and 500 in volume_ml.</label><label><input type="checkbox"/>C4\'s normalized field is empty; its missing unit is explicitly recorded.</label><label><input type="checkbox"/>C5 keeps 100 g and stays outside the volume subtotal.</label><label><input type="checkbox"/>The subtotal states 1500 mL and names the two excluded rows.</label><label><input type="checkbox"/>After any correction, I saved, reopened and checked the affected row again.</label></div><p>For a second check, sort by item name and verify that C2\'s value moves with C2, rather than remaining beside another row. If the destination strips leading IDs, converts an empty field to zero or loses the status column, repair that saved version and repeat the comparison. Our answer key gives you a known reference for these actions.</p></div>\n<h2>Money needs a confirmed code, not a volume conversion</h2><p>The liter rule above has a fixed factor. A mixed-currency table needs a different decision: first identify each currency from its source, then calculate separate subtotals. An exchange calculation would also require a specified rate and date. Do not infer USD from a dollar sign or carry the liter-to-milliliter rule into a money column.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source says</th><th>What to preserve</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.5 L</td><td>0.5 and L</td><td>Use the checked 1000 mL/L factor</td></tr><tr><td>CAD 8.00</td><td>8.00 and CAD</td><td>Keep a CAD subtotal; no rate supplied</td></tr><tr><td>$5.00; currency missing</td><td>5.00 and UNKNOWN</td><td>Ask for the currency code</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/currency-code-route.svg" width="760" height="540" loading="lazy" alt="Preserve source amounts and currency codes, group confirmed codes and keep a missing currency unresolved."/><figcaption>A fixed unit conversion and a currency conversion need different evidence.</figcaption></figure><p>Try the <a href="/ai-tools/keep-currency-codes-in-ai-tables/">six-row currency practice</a> to check ambiguous symbols, negative refunds and separate subtotals.</p><h2>Questions about mixed units</h2><details><summary>Can I replace a missing unit with the column heading?</summary><p>Only if the source explicitly establishes that heading for that value. C4\'s practice note does not. Keep it unresolved and ask for the unit; surrounding rows are not confirmation.</p></details><details><summary>Can I add grams and milliliters if they describe the same product?</summary><p>They measure different quantities. A mass-to-volume calculation would need additional applicable information, such as a specified density and its conditions. This exercise supplies none, so keep the mass row separate.</p></details><details><summary>Is a correct subtotal enough to pass the table?</summary><p>No. Check each source row too. Two wrong conversions can offset one another while producing the expected total. The original number, unit and source ID let you inspect the individual calculation.</p></details>\n<p><a href="/ai-tools/check-free-ai-export/">Check the file\'s export and editing route</a> · <a href="/ai-tools/keep-zero-blank-unknown/">Keep missing values separate from zero</a> · <a href="/ai-tools/ask-about-missing-details/">Ask for one missing detail</a></p>\n<p>Reviewed October 12, 2026. Cedar notes, worked revisions, checks and diagrams are our original practice material. Official references establish units and function behavior.</p></article>'
def publish_unit_guide(page, cfg):
    page("/ai-tools/check-units-in-ai-tables/", "Check units in AI tables before adding numbers | "+cfg["brand"], "Keep source values and units, normalize compatible rows and verify a labelled subtotal with a five-row practice file.", BODY, lastmod="2026-10-12")

